ed  everywhere  in fact uh the dulles brothers were  founders of the council on foreign  relations and while i was researching  that aspect of the book  i realized that the council on foreign  relations actually has a slogan i hadn't  realized this but if you look on their  magazine foreign affairs you'll see they  have a logo and the logo is a flying  horse it's the pegasus figure  and then there's a little scribble in  the circle which i always thought was  just chicken scratch i never really paid  attention to it  but i looked at it more closely as i was  working on this book and i see that it's  it's a latin word  and the word is  ubiqui means everywhere  that is the answer to these great  questions where does the united states  have vital interests where must the  united states be involved where do we  have to have operations underway all the  time  everywhere  that was the  concept that guided the dulles brothers  and the foreign policy establishment  that ruled this country really for the  first half of the 20th century right  into the 1960s what's remarkable about  this elite is how small it was in those  days american foreign policy was really  the province of a very small group of  people they all went to the same schools  they were all white protestant males  they were all wealthy they all worked  for the same law firms and the same  investment banks they all had the same  outlook on the world  it was a very inbred group and they all  shared the same view of the world  the dulles brothers were perhaps the  ultimate exemplars of that view  now  in my book i don't just talk about the  dulles brothers as political figures i'm  trying to understand them as individuals  and that's why i take their story all  the way back to early childhood and  before  the dulles brothers were really  vessels of american history  so let me tell you a little bit about  the forces  that shaped them because i think these  are also forces that shaped our country  the dulles brothe