ng trips  that he took were very influential in  pushing him towards the career that he  had in espionage and covert action  because he said you catch the fish then  you play him and you move him and you  try to slowly reel him in until he gets  to the point where he's almost grateful  to be caught  this was the lesson that alan dallas  took from his fishing trips when he was  a young boy  so that became the second force that  shaped the dulles brothers their  introduction to the highest levels of  the american elite at a very young age  the third force came later in the dulles  brothers  lives  both of them went to work  for  the unique  corporate law firm of sullivan and  cromwell in fact john foster dulles the  older brother was the managing partner  of sullivan and cromwell for 30 years  alan dulles also worked there for  decades  uh now sullivan and cromwell  it was an old normal law firm  they could draft a contract for you if  you wanted or even represent you in a  courtroom but that was not what they  existed to do  sullivan and cromwell had a specialty  and their specialty was  pressuring small and weak countries to  do what big american corporations wanted  them to do  that's why  almost every big american multinational  corporation  hired sullivan and cromwell and  in effect the dulles brothers to defend  them  john foster dulles had just gone to work  for the company as a young clerk fifty  dollars a month  which of course grandfather foster  generously supplemented  when he was given his first assignment  and it's a classic one because it shows  exactly what  sullivan and cromwell did  an american agricultural products  company had hired sullivan and cromwell  to do  exactly the kind of thing that sullivan  and cromwell was famous for  this company wanted to export flour to  guyana  but guyana had tariffs in order to  protect the domestic agricultural  industry  so sullivan and cromwell was asked by  this american company to see if they  could make those tariffs go away  john foster dulles got the job  he went to guyana  uh he made the guyanese leaders an offer  they couldn't refuse  not sure it included a horse's head but  we don't know uh and when he came home  there were no more tariffs on american  flour coming into guyana  it was only a few years after that that  foster dulles was given another job that  really reflected what sullivan and  cromwell could do uh that company that  law firm represented most of the  american corporations that were active  in cuba almost all the big american  sugar companies and fruit companies that  were active in cuba plus the owners of  the cuban railroad lines and the owners  of cement companies and several mines in  cuba were all  sullivan and cromwell clients  in the second decade  of the 20th century  an election was held in cuba and the  incumbent conservative party which had  been happy to give to american  corporations  everything they wanted was defeated  the liberal party won the election but  the conservatives refused to give up  office so the liberals began a rebellion  in order to force uh the country to  observe the result of the election  the american corporations were terrified  by this because the liberal party was  committed to land reform and of course  the way they were going to get the land  they wanted to give out to peasants was  they were going to get it from the big  american sugar companies and the fruit  companies and they wanted to erect a  tariff wall around cuba to promote  domestic industry so in short the  liberal party was a nightmare terror  figure for american corporate interests  represented by sullivan and cromwell  john foster dulles was given the job  figure out what to do about this problem  the next morning he got on the train in  new york and he went to washington d.c  he went to see the secretary of state  who happened to be  uncle burt  he told uncle bert  we should send two warships to cuba  we should both go to the eastern end of  th