cess  in protecting the interests of the big  american corporations that were their  clients  they were able to guarantee their  clients if you have interests in a  foreign country and you hire us  your interests will be safe  and it was that gold standard that kept  the dulles brothers so rich and kept  sullivan and cromwell uh so desirable  for american corporations  in 1951  the dulles brothers suffered two  terrible blows  blows of the kind they had never  suffered before or ever even imagined  the first one happened in guatemala  the elected government of guatemala  under president jokobo arbenz with the  approval of the elected democratic  congress of guatemala  decided on a land reform act  that land reform act has its principal  clause  those who own huge amounts of land  and aren't using most of it  have to sell the unused land  to the government and the government  will then slice up this land and give  little parcels out to  landless peasants  the biggest land owner in guatemala was  united fruit the banana company  it had over half a million acres of  unused land  i think one of the real reasons they  wanted to keep that land was they didn't  want any other banana company in there  to compete with them  so  this land reform act was passed the  dulles brothers protested vigorously so  did the united states government but  there was nothing the dulles brothers  could do as private lawyers they had to  sit back and watch one of their most  important clients suffer a terrible blow  in guatemala it wasn't just the united  fruit company  united fruit had two other subsidiary or  associated companies that helped  solidify their control over guatemala  they owned irka the central american  railroad company that owned all the rail  lines in guatemala and they owned the  electric company that produced all the  electricity in guatemala all three of  these companies were sullivan and  cromwell clients they were clients of  the dulles brothers um and so was the  schr